--- MESSAGE ---
A dyn's result is its body element named value, and the = marker retires

Dev, on the `=` line a block dyn ended with: the result was originally conceived as part of the body,
which is [BodyElement] — but no body element kind is a result, so a value expression after the last
statement is outside the body syntax, and `=` was the marker that exception needed. Dynamic was meant
to add polymorphism, not a body syntax of its own. And a result has to have a type.

The fix was already in the tree. TypeElement is Primal(Json), so every element carries a `value`, and
a factory body sets an instance's value with `fix value <type> <expr>` — draketarget/DomainLine does
exactly that. A dyn body does the same for the method's result. It is identified BY NAME, so nothing
has to be last and no expression grammar is needed to bound it. That also answers the type: the
`fix value` line carries one, and it must agree with the dyn's — which is the point at which
`valueType` being a string of Scala stops being tolerable, and is why the next steps are the call
syntax and value types as trees.

    dyn load TypeDefinition [
      parameters
        par path String
      body
        loc source scala.io.BufferedSource scala.io.Source.fromFile(path)
        loc text String try source.mkString finally source.close()
        fix value TypeDefinition io.circe.parser.parse(text).flatMap(_.as[TypeDefinition]).getOrElse(TypeDefinition.Null)
    ]

The `body` head follows `parameters`, as it does in a factory and as Dev originally conceived it; the
dyn had dropped the word and run its statements straight after the parameters. Case branches carry
their result the same way. A block dyn and a case branch carry no value of their own any more; the
leaf dyn `dyn x T expr` keeps its value field, being shorthand for a body with one `value` element.

Twenty results in seven definitions moved into their bodies, and an eighth definition's effect-only
block (ContentSink's factory `write`) gained the head — caught by the first run, which the `=`-line
sweep had missed because that block had no result. The projected Scala does not change: the
engine partitions the `value` element out of the statements and writes it where it wrote the `=`
expression — the same block, the same final line — and DracoGenTest holds it to that. The `=` token
stays reserved for the named-argument marker until the call syntax lands.

